    "text": "# Appointment Reminder Service\n\n## Scope\n\nA small service that, given an appointment (patient, provider, start time, channel), delivers a reminder at a configured lead time (e.g., 24h and 1h before). Assume SMS/email via a provider API. Assume a Postgres database and a pool of workers.\n\n## Data model\n\n```\nappointments(id, patient_id, start_at_utc, tz, status, version)\nreminders(id, appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc, state, attempt, next_attempt_at,\n          lease_until, provider_msg_id, sent_at, idem_key UNIQUE, updated_at)\n```\n\n- `start_at_utc` is the source of truth; `tz` is stored for display and for computing \"9am local\" style rules.\n- `idem_key = hash(appointment_id, channel, offset)` \u2014 one row per logical reminder, so re-scheduling the same reminder is idempotent.\n- `state \u2208 {pending, leased, sent, failed, cancelled}`.\n\n## Scheduling and time zones\n\n- All scheduling math is done in UTC. `fire_at_utc = start_at_utc - lead_time`.\n- The appointment's `tz` is used only to render the message body (\"Your appointment is at 9:00 AM PDT\") and to interpret rules like \"don't send between 9pm\u20137am local,\" which are evaluated against the patient's tz at send time.\n- DST: because we store an absolute instant, a reminder for a 9am appointment the day after a DST shift still fires at the correct wall-clock offset. We never store \"9am local\" as the trigger; we store the instant.\n- If the patient's tz changes (travel), we do **not** retroactively move the reminder",
